SELECT 'd4d34fff-197d-5505-80cb-59473220feb2','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='d4d34fff-197d-5505-80cb-59473220feb2' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'df40666b-3169-5b61-ab33-9c80053d4a1d','Snare-vent microphone','A microphone placed at or just outside the snare shell''s air vent (the small hole in the shell), capturing the pressure pulse of air forced through the vent when the head is struck. This position emphasises attack and body while offering strong isolation from cymbals and other kit elements.','Every snare drum has a small air hole in its side, called a vent. When you hit the drum, air puffs out of this hole. A vent microphone sits at that hole to catch a punchy attack. It also picks up little spill from the cymbals.','It exists to capture a focused, punchy snare attack with high isolation, useful when overhead and top mics pick up too much cymbal bleed.','The engineer positions a small-diaphragm or miniature mic near the vent hole and uses it to add punch or as a low-bleed alternative to a top mic.','Drum miking',ARRAY['Snare-shell microphone','Snare-bottom polarity inversion','Bleed (spill)','Miniature microphone','Snare-wire balance']::text[],ARRAY['Sealing the vent with the mic or tape, which can affect the drum''s tuning and resonance.','Expecting a full, balanced snare tone from the vent alone; it favours attack and body and is normally blended.','UNSAFE: The vent releases a sharp pressure pulse at very high SPL; protect hearing and monitor at safe levels, as snare peaks can exceed safe exposure limits.']::text[],ARRAY['Adding punch to a snare with heavy cymbal bleed on a loud stage.','Recording a tight, isolated snare track intended for sample replacement.','Blending a vent mic under the top mic to fatten a studio snare.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='df40666b-3169-5b61-ab33-9c80053d4a1d' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Snare-vent microphone'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'df40666b-3169-5b61-ab33-9c80053d4a1d','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='df40666b-3169-5b61-ab33-9c80053d4a1d' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '992fa40d-74d4-5c09-8875-9d1c977b7a82','Snare-wire balance','The relative balance between the sound of the snare wires (the snappy buzz from the strainer-mounted wires against the bottom head) and the fundamental tone of the drum. It is set through microphone choice and placement—especially the blend of top-head and bottom-head mics—and through the tension of the snare wires themselves.','A snare drum has metal wires stretched under it that make its buzzy snap. Snare-wire balance is how loud that snap is next to the drum''s main tone. Engineers set it with mic placement and the mix of the top and bottom mics. Wire tightness also changes it.','It exists to control how much crisp snap versus deep body the recorded snare has, matching the drum to the song.','An engineer raises the bottom-snare mic for more wire snap or the top mic for more body, and may retension the snares to fine-tune the buzz.','Drum miking',ARRAY['Snare-bottom polarity inversion','Snare-wire choke','Snare-vent microphone','Noise gate','Bleed (spill)']::text[],ARRAY['Pushing the bottom-snare mic too hot, so wire buzz and sympathetic rattle dominate the sound.','Forgetting to check top/bottom polarity when adjusting the balance, which can thin out the drum.','Over-tightening the snares to reduce buzz, which chokes the drum''s resonance instead of balancing it.']::text[],ARRAY['Dialling in a crisp backbeat for a pop production.','Reducing excessive snare buzz that rings under tom and bass hits.','Balancing snap and body when blending top and bottom snare mics.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='992fa40d-74d4-5c09-8875-9d1c977b7a82' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Snare-wire balance'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '992fa40d-74d4-5c09-8875-9d1c977b7a82','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='992fa40d-74d4-5c09-8875-9d1c977b7a82' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'ff60d6a1-5b6f-5e20-a7a1-03dd631a88e0','Snare-wire choke','A condition in which the snare wires are tensioned too tightly against the bottom (resonant) head, clamping it and deadening the drum''s resonance, sustain and natural wire response. The result is a short, dry, ''choked'' sound with reduced sensitivity to soft strokes and buzz rolls.','Snare wires are tightened with a lever called the throw-off. If they are too tight, they press hard on the drum and kill its ring. The snare then sounds dead and stiff. Quiet hits barely make the wires respond.','Recognising choke matters because correct snare-wire tension sets the balance between a lively, resonant snare and a tight, controlled one.','When a snare sounds lifeless, the technician backs off the strainer tension until the wires respond freely to soft notes, then fine-tunes for the desired snap and sustain.','Snare technique',ARRAY['Snare-wire balance','Snare-bottom polarity inversion','Snare-vent microphone','Resonant head','Throw-off (strainer)']::text[],ARRAY['Cranking snare tension to kill unwanted buzz, which chokes the drum instead of solving the tuning or gating issue.','Blaming the microphone for a dead snare sound when the wires are actually choked.','Confusing a choked snare with a well-damped one; damping controls ring, while choking clamps the resonant head and kills response.']::text[],ARRAY['Diagnosing a lifeless snare during a studio drum-tuning session.','Reviving soft-note response for a jazz or funk groove.','Deciding whether a dead snare is choked wires or over-damping.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='ff60d6a1-5b6f-5e20-a7a1-03dd631a88e0' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Snare-wire choke'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'ff60d6a1-5b6f-5e20-a7a1-03dd631a88e0','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='ff60d6a1-5b6f-5e20-a7a1-03dd631a88e0' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '923d83f2-5ad0-5281-9ecc-306dadd41fe3','Sound-hole avoidance','A microphone-placement guideline for acoustic guitar (and similar bodied instruments) that avoids pointing a close microphone directly into the sound hole, where the body''s Helmholtz air resonance produces an exaggerated, boomy low-frequency buildup roughly in the 80-120 Hz region. The mic is instead aimed toward the neck/body joint, around the 12th to 14th fret, for a more balanced tone.','An acoustic guitar''s round hole pushes out a lot of boomy low sound. Point a mic straight at it and the recording turns muddy. Instead, aim the mic where the neck meets the body, around the 12th fret. This gives a clearer, more even tone.','It exists to avoid the muddy low-frequency buildup radiating from the sound hole and to capture a balanced blend of string detail and body warmth.','The engineer starts with the mic about 15-30 cm from the guitar, aimed at the 12th-14th fret rather than the hole, then angles toward or away from the hole to add or reduce low end.','Acoustic guitar miking',ARRAY['Proximity effect','3:1 rule','Off-axis placement','String-body shadowing','Helmholtz resonance']::text[],ARRAY['Pointing the mic straight at the sound hole for maximum level, then fighting the boominess with heavy EQ afterwards.','Placing a directional mic very close, so proximity effect adds even more low-end.','Assuming one position suits every guitar; body size and tuning shift where the balanced spot lies.']::text[],ARRAY['Miking a singer-songwriter''s acoustic guitar for a clear, balanced tone.','Reducing boom on a large dreadnought guitar in a small room.','Positioning a clip-on mic on an acoustic guitar for live performance.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='923d83f2-5ad0-5281-9ecc-306dadd41fe3' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Sound-hole avoidance'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '923d83f2-5ad0-5281-9ecc-306dadd41fe3','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='923d83f2-5ad0-5281-9ecc-306dadd41fe3' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '7d04e284-a969-5eef-a299-b2194ce44fb5','Speaker-cone traversal','The technique of moving a close microphone across the face of a guitar or bass loudspeaker cone to shape tone, exploiting the fact that a cone radiates more high-frequency content near its centre (dust cap) and progressively warmer, darker tone toward its outer edge. Position along this radius—plus on-axis versus off-axis angle—sets the captured brightness.','A guitar amp''s speaker sounds brighter in the middle and warmer at the edge. Moving the mic across the speaker, from centre to rim, changes how bright it sounds. Engineers slide the mic along the cone to find the tone they want. Tilting the mic to the side also softens the highs.','It exists to dial in an amplifier''s recorded tone by mic placement alone, before reaching for EQ.','With a dynamic mic close to the grille, the engineer moves it from the dust-cap centre toward the cone edge, and tilts it off-axis, until the guitar sits right in the mix.','Amp miking',ARRAY['Off-axis placement','Proximity effect','On-axis response','Cabinet miking','3:1 rule']::text[],ARRAY['Only ever miking the dead centre, then removing the resulting harshness with EQ instead of moving the mic.','Ignoring on-axis versus off-axis angle, which changes brightness as much as the cone position does.','UNSAFE: Guitar cabinets reach very high SPL right at the grille; do not put your ear at the speaker to audition, and protect your hearing during loud amp sessions.']::text[],ARRAY['Finding the sweet spot on a 4x12 guitar cabinet with a dynamic mic.','Taming a harsh amp tone by moving off the dust cap toward the cone edge.','Positioning two mics on a bass cabinet and checking their blend.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='7d04e284-a969-5eef-a299-b2194ce44fb5' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Speaker-cone traversal'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '7d04e284-a969-5eef-a299-b2194ce44fb5','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='7d04e284-a969-5eef-a299-b2194ce44fb5' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'bb075cbb-b0e8-5087-b4e1-c45e4844ae9b','Spot-microphone time alignment','The practice of delaying a spot (accent) microphone so that its signal, which arrives earlier because the mic is close to the source, is time-aligned with the later arrival of that source at the main/stereo array. The delay is set roughly to the extra distance the sound travels to the main array divided by the speed of sound (about 343 m/s at 20 C), preserving natural depth and localisation and reducing comb filtering when the spot is blended in.','In orchestra recording, a main pair of mics sits far back and close ''spot'' mics sit near single instruments. Sound reaches a close mic first and the main pair later. Delaying the spot mic to match the main pair keeps the image natural. It also avoids a hollow, phasey sound.','It exists to restore correct arrival timing between close and distant mics, keeping instruments localised where they belong and preventing tonal coloration from comb filtering.','The engineer measures the distance from each spot mic to the main array, converts it to a delay (about 2.9 milliseconds per metre), and applies that delay to the spot channel.','Mic time alignment',ARRAY['Comb filtering','3:1 rule','Main array (stereo pair)','Haas effect','Speed of sound']::text[],ARRAY['Blending loud spot mics with no delay, causing comb filtering and pulling the image toward the close mic.','Using polarity inversion to fix what is actually a timing error; only a delay corrects arrival time.','Over-delaying past the main array''s arrival, which smears transients and muddies depth.']::text[],ARRAY['Delaying a woodwind spot mic to match a Decca-tree main array in an orchestral session.','Aligning a close tom mic to the drum overheads to tighten the kit''s transient.','Time-aligning solo-instrument accent mics to a main pair in a live classical broadcast.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='bb075cbb-b0e8-5087-b4e1-c45e4844ae9b' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Spot-microphone time alignment'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'bb075cbb-b0e8-5087-b4e1-c45e4844ae9b','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='bb075cbb-b0e8-5087-b4e1-c45e4844ae9b' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'de1cb708-2006-5963-ae2e-bc54cea5db88','Stacked-Sibilance Control','Techniques for managing the buildup of sibilance—the concentrated high-frequency energy of ''s'', ''sh'', ''ch'' and ''t'' sounds—that occurs when multiple vocal takes are layered (stacked) together. Because sibilant peaks from each take add up, a stack can become harsh; control is applied by de-essing individual takes before combining them, and/or de-essing or high-frequency-limiting the summed stack.','Singers often record the same part several times to make it sound bigger. The sharp ''s'' and ''sh'' sounds add up across all those takes and get harsh. Stacked-sibilance control tames that harshness. Usually you de-ess each take before mixing them together.','It exists to keep layered or doubled vocals smooth and easy to listen to, preventing the piercing sibilance that results when many takes stack up.','An engineer places a de-esser on each vocal layer, or on the vocal group bus, and tunes it to the sibilant frequency so the combined stack stays smooth.','Sibilance control',ARRAY['De-esser','Sibilance','Vocal doubling','Sidechain high-pass filtering','Multiband compression']::text[],ARRAY['De-essing only the final vocal bus, so already-summed sibilant peaks are hard to tame without dulling the whole stack.','Over-de-essing each layer until the vocals lisp and lose clarity.','Blaming the microphone for harshness that actually comes from stacking many sibilant takes.']::text[],ARRAY['Smoothing a wall of doubled backing vocals in a pop chorus.','Taming harsh ''s'' sounds on a quadruple-tracked lead vocal.','De-essing individual harmony layers before printing a vocal group.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='de1cb708-2006-5963-ae2e-bc54cea5db88' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Stacked-Sibilance Control'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'de1cb708-2006-5963-ae2e-bc54cea5db88','c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='de1cb708-2006-5963-ae2e-bc54cea5db88' AND gt.achievement_id='c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '25293ec8-3f79-5ea1-8c22-eee39b51524d','String-body shadowing','The tonal coloration that results when the body of a string instrument (violin, cello, guitar) partly blocks or ''shadows'' sound radiating in certain directions, so a close microphone placed off the instrument''s main radiation axis captures an uneven frequency balance. Because a string instrument radiates different frequencies in different directions and is voiced to be heard at a distance, close placement can pick up a shadowed, coloured version of its full tone.','A violin or guitar sends different pitches out in different directions. Its own body blocks some of that sound, like a wall casting a shadow. A close mic in the wrong spot hears this shadowed tone, which sounds uneven. Moving the mic finds a fuller, more even sound.','Understanding body shadowing helps a technician place a mic where the instrument''s full range reaches it, rather than a spot that misses certain frequencies.','When a close-miked string instrument sounds thin or boxy, the engineer moves the mic around the body and out a little to escape the shadowed zone and capture a more even tone.','String miking',ARRAY['Sound-hole avoidance','Proximity effect','Off-axis placement','Polar pattern','Spot-microphone time alignment']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming a single close spot captures the whole instrument, ignoring that the body radiates differently in each direction.','Miking too close, so shadowing and proximity effect both distort the balance.','Fixing a shadowed, uneven tone only with EQ instead of first repositioning the mic.']::text[],ARRAY['Repositioning a violin spot mic that sounds thin and boxy.','Choosing where to clip a mic on a cello for an even tone.','Miking an acoustic guitar body to avoid an uneven, coloured capture.']::text[],'c1bd3df7-1ba9-416e-8ebd-538a91ac2517'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='25293ec8-3f79-5ea1-8c22-eee39b51524d' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('String-body shadowing'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
