SELECT 'a97177ef-7ec6-5df7-91fb-7e3eab6360f8','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='a97177ef-7ec6-5df7-91fb-7e3eab6360f8' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '2aa62093-b8da-5f5f-aa63-5c13b7075601','Early-reflection equalization','Applying equalization specifically to the early-reflection portion of a reverb—the first sparse, discrete reflections that arrive after the direct sound—separately from the late tail. Shaping their spectrum controls perceived room surface, brightness, and clarity without altering the character of the diffuse decay.','A reverb starts with a few early echoes, then a smooth tail. Early-reflection equalization means shaping the tone of just those first echoes. You can make them brighter or darker. This changes the sense of the room without changing the long tail.','It exists to tune the tone and clarity of the early reflections—which carry cues about room size and surface—independently of the reverb tail, so a space can be shaped to fit the mix.','An engineer rolls off highs on the early reflections to soften a harsh room, or cuts low-mids to reduce boxiness, while leaving the tail''s EQ untouched.','Reverb processing',ARRAY['Early reflections','Early-reflection reverb','Equalization','Reverb tail','Pre-delay','Room simulation']::text[],ARRAY['EQ-ing the whole reverb bus and assuming only the early reflections changed.','Boosting early-reflection highs so much that the reverb turns harsh and sibilant.','Ignoring that early reflections carry room-size cues, so heavy EQ makes the space sound unnatural.']::text[],ARRAY['Softening bright early reflections on a vocal reverb.','Cutting boxy low-mids out of a room simulation.','Shaping early reflections to make a plate sound like a smaller, warmer space.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='2aa62093-b8da-5f5f-aa63-5c13b7075601' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Early-reflection equalization'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '2aa62093-b8da-5f5f-aa63-5c13b7075601','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='2aa62093-b8da-5f5f-aa63-5c13b7075601' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '26511b92-77c7-5511-a034-3a931d355941','Early-reflection reverb','A reverb program or algorithm that generates only the early reflections—the sparse, discrete echoes that arrive shortly after the direct sound—without a dense late decay tail. Built from a tapped delay line modeling reflections off nearby surfaces, it adds a sense of space, size, and placement while leaving the source dry-sounding and uncluttered.','When a sound plays in a room, a few clear echoes arrive first, before the long tail. An early-reflection reverb makes just those first echoes and no long tail. It adds a feeling of space and size. The sound still stays clear and close.','It exists to add spatial size and depth cues without the wash of a full reverb tail, useful for placing a source in a room while keeping the mix clean.','An engineer uses an early-reflection-only program to push a dry vocal or drum slightly back and give it room context, or ahead of a tail reverb to thicken its onset.','Reverb type',ARRAY['Early reflections','Early-reflection equalization','Pre-delay','Reverb tail','Room simulation','Tapped delay line']::text[],ARRAY['Expecting a long decay; an early-reflection reverb produces short discrete echoes, not a lush tail.','Setting reflection times so long that they sound like a separate slap echo instead of room ambience.','Piling on level until the early reflections comb-filter and hollow out the source.']::text[],ARRAY['Adding room size to a close-miked drum without a long tail.','Placing a dry vocal in a subtle space to sit better in the mix.','Layering early reflections ahead of a hall tail to define its onset.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='26511b92-77c7-5511-a034-3a931d355941' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Early-reflection reverb'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '26511b92-77c7-5511-a034-3a931d355941','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='26511b92-77c7-5511-a034-3a931d355941' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '52bbef84-f991-5818-8333-4d2f1f1754ec','Echo density','In reverberation, echo density is the number of reflections (echoes) arriving per unit time, which increases as a room''s impulse response evolves from sparse early reflections to a dense, statistically diffuse tail. Adequate echo density—so individual echoes are no longer separable—is essential to a natural, non-fluttery artificial reverb.','Echo density is how many echoes arrive each second inside a reverb. Early on there are only a few. Over time they pile up until you cannot hear them one by one. A good reverb builds up enough density so the tail sounds smooth, not grainy.','It exists as a key measure of reverb quality: too low and the tail sounds grainy or flutters; high enough and the reflections blur into a smooth, natural decay.','When a reverb tail sounds grainy or metallic, an engineer raises a diffusion or density control, which increases echo density so the decay smooths out.','Reverb parameter',ARRAY['Diffusion','Reverb tail','Early reflections','Reverberation time (RT60)','Flutter echo','Comb filtering','Density buildup']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing echo density with decay time; density is echoes per second, not how long the tail lasts.','Leaving density too low, so the tail flutters or sounds grainy on transient material.','Assuming more diffusion is always better, when some material wants sparser, more defined reflections.']::text[],ARRAY['Smoothing a metallic-sounding reverb tail on a snare.','Choosing a high-density algorithm for dense orchestral material.','Diagnosing a grainy artificial reverb by increasing diffusion.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='52bbef84-f991-5818-8333-4d2f1f1754ec' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Echo density'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '52bbef84-f991-5818-8333-4d2f1f1754ec','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='52bbef84-f991-5818-8333-4d2f1f1754ec' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '70889a83-4de7-5ad2-a64c-c8f6209b4eff','Envelope-controlled delay','A delay whose parameters—commonly feedback, wet level, or delay time—are modulated in real time by an envelope follower that tracks the amplitude of the input signal. Louder playing can, for example, reduce the delay level or feedback and let it rise as the source decays, so the effect responds dynamically to performance.','An envelope follower watches how loud the input is moment to moment. An envelope-controlled delay uses that to change its own settings live. For example, the echoes stay low while you play hard. They swell up as the sound dies away.','It exists to make a delay respond to playing dynamics automatically, keeping repeats out of the way during loud passages and letting them bloom in quiet moments.','A player routes an envelope follower to the delay''s level or feedback so hard picking keeps echoes back, then the tails swell as notes decay, giving expressive, dynamic ambience.','Delay modulation',ARRAY['Envelope follower','Ducked delay','Feedback (delay)','Sidechain (key input)','Modulation','Delay send']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing an envelope-controlled delay with a simple ducked delay; the envelope can modulate time or feedback, not just level.','Setting the envelope response too fast, so the delay level jumps unnaturally with every note.','Modulating delay time heavily, which causes obvious pitch glitches on sustained notes.']::text[],ARRAY['Letting guitar echoes swell only as chords ring out and fade.','Keeping delay out of the way during loud verses and blooming in quiet ones.','Adding dynamic, playing-responsive ambience to a synth lead.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='70889a83-4de7-5ad2-a64c-c8f6209b4eff' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Envelope-controlled delay'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
