WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='a25b31cb-2b73-5d79-bb8c-612f3f330f35' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '9ea7ca5a-866f-5dcd-979e-390e31c87ec2','Reverb hold','A momentary or latching function that sets the reverb''s decay time to infinite while engaged, so the reverberant energy stops decaying and the current tail is sustained for as long as the control is held; releasing it returns the reverb to its normal decay. Implementations vary in whether new input continues to feed the sustained field.','Hold keeps the reverb ringing while you press it. The tail does not fade away. Let go and the reverb fades like normal again. It is often a hands-on control you tap during a part.','It lets a performer or engineer sustain a reverb wash on demand for as long as needed, as a live, gestural control rather than a fixed decay setting.','A guitarist or keyboardist stamps a hold footswitch to sustain ambience under a chord, or an engineer automates it to swell a tail under a transition.','Reverb parameter',ARRAY['Reverb freeze','Decay time (RT60)','Reverb regeneration','Reverb tail','Infinite reverb']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing hold with freeze; on some units hold keeps taking input while freeze blocks it, giving different results.','Leaving hold latched and building an unintended pile-up of overlapping notes in the sustained tail.','UNSAFE: Monitoring long, held tails at high sound pressure levels for extended periods; per OSHA and NIOSH guidance prolonged exposure above roughly 85 dBA risks hearing damage, so keep levels down.']::text[],ARRAY['Sustaining an ambient chord under a live keyboard part.','Swelling a reverb tail into the downbeat of a new section.','Holding a pad texture while switching patches on stage.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='9ea7ca5a-866f-5dcd-979e-390e31c87ec2' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb hold'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '9ea7ca5a-866f-5dcd-979e-390e31c87ec2','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='9ea7ca5a-866f-5dcd-979e-390e31c87ec2' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '88c1609f-971b-5313-a3d4-ef5d284c0abf','Reverb input ducking','A technique in which the level feeding a reverb, or the reverb return, is automatically attenuated by a sidechain keyed from the dry source, so the reverb dips while the source plays and swells back up in the gaps, keeping the dry signal clear while still providing ambience.','Input ducking lowers the reverb while the dry sound plays. In the gaps between words or notes, the reverb rises back up. It keeps vocals clear but still adds space. A sidechain does this by itself.','It exists to keep a source intelligible and up front while still using generous reverb, by pushing the wet sound out of the way whenever the dry signal is present.','An engineer sidechains a compressor on the reverb send or return, keyed by the lead vocal, so the reverb ducks under the words and blooms between phrases.','Reverb technique',ARRAY['Sidechain compression','Reverb send','Reverb return','Ducking','Reverb predelay']::text[],ARRAY['Setting release too fast, so the reverb pumps audibly instead of swelling smoothly between phrases.','Keying the ducker from the wet signal instead of the dry source, which defeats the effect.','Using so much ducking that the reverb disappears and the source sounds dry and disconnected.']::text[],ARRAY['Keeping a lead vocal clear while still using a large reverb.','Ducking a drum reverb so the transients stay punchy.','Pop or EDM production where a big wet tail must not bury the vocal.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='88c1609f-971b-5313-a3d4-ef5d284c0abf' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb input ducking'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '88c1609f-971b-5313-a3d4-ef5d284c0abf','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='88c1609f-971b-5313-a3d4-ef5d284c0abf' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '4cca546f-ceb5-5b1b-8011-57e01062c787','Reverb onset shaping','Adjusting the attack portion of a reverb''s amplitude envelope, that is, how quickly and with what contour the reverberant energy builds after the direct sound, using controls such as Shape and Spread, an envelope or gate, or volume automation on the return, rather than altering the decay tail.','Onset shaping changes how the reverb begins, not how it ends. You can make the reverb fade in slowly or jump in fast. Tools like Shape, Spread, or a gate do this. It sets the reverb''s attack.','It exists to control how the reverb enters the mix, from soft swells to instant ambience, so the effect supports the arrangement instead of clouding the source''s attack.','An engineer uses a reverb''s Shape and Spread controls, or automates the return level, to fade the tail in gently on a ballad or snap it in tight on a rhythmic part.','Reverb technique',ARRAY['Reverb shape','Reverb spread','Reverb predelay','Gated reverb','Early reflections']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming only decay time matters and ignoring how the reverb builds at the start.','Confusing onset shaping with predelay; predelay delays the whole tail, while shaping changes how it grows.']::text[],ARRAY['Creating a slow reverb swell under a sustained pad or vocal.','Tightening a snare''s ambience so the room does not smear the hit.','Designing a cinematic riser that blooms into a scene change.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='4cca546f-ceb5-5b1b-8011-57e01062c787' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb onset shaping'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '4cca546f-ceb5-5b1b-8011-57e01062c787','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='4cca546f-ceb5-5b1b-8011-57e01062c787' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '240342f9-d5c7-5f87-9500-c9ae9b24c75e','Reverb predelay','The adjustable time gap, set in milliseconds, between the arrival of the dry (direct) signal and the onset of the reverb''s early reflections and tail. Typical values run from a few milliseconds up to roughly 100-150 ms; longer predelays imply larger perceived spaces and help separate the source from its reverb.','Predelay is the short gap before the reverb starts. The dry sound plays first. A few thousandths of a second later, the reverb comes in. A longer gap makes the room feel bigger and keeps the source clear.','It exists to separate the direct sound from its reverberation, preserving clarity and attack while still adding space, and to suggest the size of the emulated room.','An engineer sets predelay so the reverb starts just after a vocal''s consonants, often syncing it to the track''s tempo to keep the mix clear.','Reverb parameter',ARRAY['Early reflections','Reverb tail','Decay time (RT60)','Reverb predelay offset','Diffusion']::text[],ARRAY['Using zero predelay on a busy vocal, so the reverb smears the attack and muddies the mix.','Setting predelay so long it sounds like a distinct slap echo rather than reverb, unless that is intended.']::text[],ARRAY['Adding a hall reverb to a lead vocal without losing clarity.','Timing predelay to the tempo so the tail blooms on the beat.','Creating front-to-back depth between instruments in a mix.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='240342f9-d5c7-5f87-9500-c9ae9b24c75e' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb predelay'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '240342f9-d5c7-5f87-9500-c9ae9b24c75e','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='240342f9-d5c7-5f87-9500-c9ae9b24c75e' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '611bfd21-8e3e-55ed-8115-3661391dab6c','Reverb predelay offset','A deliberate adjustment of a reverb''s predelay away from a reference point, such as an offset from a tempo-synced value or a difference in predelay set between separate reverb sends, used to fine-tune the timing of the reverb relative to the dry signal and to other reverbs in the mix.','Predelay offset means nudging the reverb''s start time. You shift it earlier or later than a set point. You can also give two reverbs different gaps. This fine-tunes how the space sits in time.','It exists to give finer timing control than a single fixed predelay, letting an engineer align or deliberately stagger reverbs so they lock to the groove or build depth layers.','An engineer offsets predelay slightly from the tempo grid, or sets a shorter gap on a near reverb and a longer one on a far reverb, to build layered depth.','Reverb technique',ARRAY['Reverb predelay','Early reflections','Reverb send','Tempo sync','Reverb spread']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing the predelay offset with the base predelay value itself.','Offsetting so far that a reverb turns into an audible separate echo when a seamless space was intended.']::text[],ARRAY['Staggering two reverbs to create near and far depth layers.','Nudging a synced predelay off the grid for a more natural feel.','Aligning a reverb''s onset with a delay throw on the same vocal.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='611bfd21-8e3e-55ed-8115-3661391dab6c' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb predelay offset'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '611bfd21-8e3e-55ed-8115-3661391dab6c','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='611bfd21-8e3e-55ed-8115-3661391dab6c' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '43e32965-b9c9-5168-9e48-cd1448464ae3','Reverb regeneration','The recirculation of a reverb''s or its associated delay''s output back into its own input through a feedback path, setting how many times the reflected energy recirculates and thus the density and length of the tail; at high settings it can drive the reverb toward infinite sustain or self-oscillation.','Regeneration feeds the reverb''s output back into itself. Each pass adds more repeats and a longer, denser tail. Turn it up and the sound can ring on and on. It is the reverb''s feedback control.','It exists to set how long and dense the reverberant or echo tail becomes, by determining how much output is fed back to recirculate through the network.','An engineer raises regeneration to lengthen and thicken a tail, or backs it off to keep the reverb short and controlled.','Reverb parameter',ARRAY['Feedback','Decay time (RT60)','Reverb freeze','Reverb tail','Comb filtering']::text[],ARRAY['UNSAFE: Pushing regeneration or feedback to self-oscillation while monitoring loud, which can cause a runaway build-up that spikes levels and endangers hearing and speakers; keep a hand on the fader and monitor at safe levels.','Confusing regeneration with decay time; both lengthen the tail but work by different means.','Setting feedback so high the tail never clears, masking the rest of the mix.']::text[],ARRAY['Lengthening a delay or reverb tail for an ambient wash.','Dialing controlled feedback for a dub-style echo effect.','Pushing toward self-oscillation for sound-design risers.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='43e32965-b9c9-5168-9e48-cd1448464ae3' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb regeneration'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '43e32965-b9c9-5168-9e48-cd1448464ae3','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='43e32965-b9c9-5168-9e48-cd1448464ae3' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '3868004c-85ef-5d3f-8d1a-9a75775f6145','Reverb return compression','Applying dynamic-range compression to the reverb return (the aux or effects channel carrying the wet signal), which evens out the level of the reverb tail, increases its perceived density and sustain, and helps the ambience sit more consistently in the mix.','This means putting a compressor on the reverb channel. It smooths out the loud and soft parts of the tail. The reverb then sounds thicker and more even. It helps the space sit steadily in the mix.','It exists to control and sustain the reverb tail''s dynamics, thickening the ambience and stopping loud reverb bursts from jumping out or quiet tails from vanishing.','An engineer inserts a compressor on the reverb return and adds moderate gain reduction so the tail blooms and sustains more evenly behind the source.','Reverb return processing',ARRAY['Reverb return','Compression','Reverb send','Sidechain compression','Makeup gain']::text[],ARRAY['Over-compressing the return so the tail pumps and breathes unnaturally.','Forgetting that compressing the return raises the reverb''s quiet decay and noise, which can add hiss.']::text[],ARRAY['Thickening a vocal reverb so the tail sustains evenly.','Adding density to a drum room reverb in a busy mix.','Gluing a wet ambience so bursts do not jump forward.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='3868004c-85ef-5d3f-8d1a-9a75775f6145' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb return compression'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '3868004c-85ef-5d3f-8d1a-9a75775f6145','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='3868004c-85ef-5d3f-8d1a-9a75775f6145' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '3091e95c-c9d5-563a-919d-918cae8fe79c','Reverb return monoing','Summing a stereo reverb return to mono, or collapsing it toward the center, so the reverberant energy occupies a single point rather than the full stereo field; used for mono compatibility, to tighten a mix, or as a deliberate creative effect.','Monoing sums the stereo reverb return to one center point. The reverb no longer spreads left and right. It sits in the middle instead. This keeps the mix tight and works when playback is mono.','It exists to ensure the reverb still sounds right when a mix folds down to mono, and to keep a spacious effect from widening or blurring the stereo image too much.','An engineer sums the reverb return to mono to check and preserve mono compatibility, or to keep a specific reverb anchored in the center of the image.','Reverb return processing',ARRAY['Reverb return','Mono compatibility','Reverb return narrowing','Phase cancellation','Stereo width']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming a wide stereo reverb always survives a mono fold-down; some collapse or partly cancel.','Monoing every reverb by default and losing width the mix actually needs.']::text[],ARRAY['Preparing a mix for club, broadcast, or phone playback that may sum to mono.','Keeping a bass or kick reverb centered and tight.','Checking mono compatibility of a wide reverb before final export.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='3091e95c-c9d5-563a-919d-918cae8fe79c' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb return monoing'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '3091e95c-c9d5-563a-919d-918cae8fe79c','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='3091e95c-c9d5-563a-919d-918cae8fe79c' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '315892ea-c960-5af0-94c2-958d29914e63','Reverb return narrowing','Reducing the stereo width of a reverb return so its image is narrower than full stereo but not fully mono, using a width or stereo-image control or mid-side processing, to keep the ambience controlled and focused within the mix.','Narrowing shrinks how wide the reverb spreads. It is not fully mono, just less wide. You pull the left and right sides closer together. This keeps the reverb focused and out of the way.','It exists to rein in an overly wide reverb, improving focus and mono compatibility while keeping some stereo spaciousness.','An engineer uses a width or mid-side control on the reverb return to narrow the image so the ambience supports the source without smearing across the whole field.','Reverb return processing',ARRAY['Reverb return monoing','Stereo width','Mid-side processing','Reverb spread','Mono compatibility']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing narrowing with monoing; narrowing keeps some width, while monoing removes it entirely.','Narrowing so far the reverb loses the spaciousness that justified using it.']::text[],ARRAY['Tightening a very wide vocal reverb that swamps the stereo field.','Improving mono compatibility while keeping some stereo depth.','Focusing a reverb around a centered lead in a dense mix.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='315892ea-c960-5af0-94c2-958d29914e63' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb return narrowing'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '315892ea-c960-5af0-94c2-958d29914e63','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='315892ea-c960-5af0-94c2-958d29914e63' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '40ff9128-3bea-58f2-8e3c-e7daa1daee7d','Reverb shape','A reverb parameter, notably on Lexicon units, that sets the contour of the reverberation envelope, that is, how the reverberant energy builds up and decays over the initial period defined by the Spread control. Low Shape values make the energy build and decay quickly; higher values give a slower buildup with more sustain.','Shape controls how the reverb''s energy rises and falls at the start. A low setting makes it burst in and fade fast. A higher setting makes it swell up slowly and sustain. It sets the reverb''s contour.','It exists, together with Spread, to sculpt the attack and early sustain of the reverb envelope, letting a designer emulate different room behaviors and reverb characters.','On a Lexicon-style reverb, an engineer adjusts Shape with Spread to make a tail swell gradually for a lush hall or snap in quickly for a tighter space.','Reverb parameter',ARRAY['Reverb spread','Reverb predelay','Early reflections','Diffusion','Reverb onset shaping']::text[],ARRAY['Adjusting Shape without Spread; the two interact and are meant to be set together.','Confusing Shape with decay time; Shape sets the initial contour, not the overall tail length.']::text[],ARRAY['Programming a custom hall on a Lexicon reverb.','Making a reverb swell in gently under a ballad vocal.','Emulating a room''s characteristic energy buildup for film.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='40ff9128-3bea-58f2-8e3c-e7daa1daee7d' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb shape'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '40ff9128-3bea-58f2-8e3c-e7daa1daee7d','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='40ff9128-3bea-58f2-8e3c-e7daa1daee7d' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '223ef181-be25-56ce-9df0-0365222eaeb7','Reverb spin','A modulation parameter, on Lexicon and similar algorithmic reverbs, that sets the rate at which the delay taps in the reverb network move or wander; this movement continuously varies the recirculating delay times to break up static resonances and metallic ringing, producing a smoother, more natural tail. It works with the Wander control, which sets how far the delays move.','Spin slowly moves the reverb''s internal echoes around. This stops the tail from settling into a harsh, metallic ring. It keeps the reverb smooth and alive. Spin sets how fast that movement happens.','It exists to add subtle motion to the reverberant field so the delays never lock into fixed patterns, which would otherwise cause coloration and ringing.','An engineer adds a little Spin, with Wander, to a lush reverb to keep long tails smooth and alive, or reduces it for a more static, focused sound.','Reverb parameter',ARRAY['Modulation','Diffusion','Comb filtering','Reverb wander','Early reflections']::text[],ARRAY['Adding too much Spin, which makes long tails sound seasick or chorused when a natural space was wanted.','Assuming Spin changes reverb length; it modulates the tail''s motion, not its decay time.']::text[],ARRAY['Smoothing a long, ringing hall tail so it sounds natural.','Adding gentle movement to a synth pad''s reverb.','Reducing metallic coloration on a bright plate reverb.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='223ef181-be25-56ce-9df0-0365222eaeb7' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb spin'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '223ef181-be25-56ce-9df0-0365222eaeb7','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='223ef181-be25-56ce-9df0-0365222eaeb7' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'c98d9f5c-5cfc-521a-ab71-650791078561','Reverb spread','A reverb parameter, notably on Lexicon units, that sets the duration of the initial contour of the reverberation envelope, the time over which the early reverberant energy builds and sustains before the main decay. Low Spread gives a rapid onset with little sustain; higher Spread lengthens the buildup and initial sustain. It works with the Shape control, which sets the contour of that energy.','Spread sets how long the reverb takes to build up at the start. A low setting makes it come in fast and short. A higher setting stretches out the buildup and sustain. It works alongside the Shape control.','It exists, with Shape, to control the time span of the reverb''s initial energy envelope, shaping how the space seems to fill in after the direct sound.','On a Lexicon-style reverb, an engineer raises Spread with Shape to make a hall bloom slowly and richly, or lowers it for a tighter, quicker onset.','Reverb parameter',ARRAY['Reverb shape','Reverb predelay','Early reflections','Diffusion','Reverb onset shaping']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing stereo spread (width) with the Lexicon Spread parameter, which controls envelope timing, not width.','Setting Spread without Shape; the two interact to define the initial envelope.']::text[],ARRAY['Programming the initial bloom of a custom hall reverb.','Making a reverb fill in slowly under a sustained vocal.','Dialing a tight, quick onset for a rhythmic reverb.']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='c98d9f5c-5cfc-521a-ab71-650791078561' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb spread'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'c98d9f5c-5cfc-521a-ab71-650791078561','415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='c98d9f5c-5cfc-521a-ab71-650791078561' AND gt.achievement_id='415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'b6d29445-7c11-5794-b925-415b39d34b35','Reverb stacking','The practice of running two or more reverb processors on a signal at once, layered in parallel or combined across sends, so their individual decays, densities, and tonal characters sum into a single composite artificial space more complex than any one unit could produce alone.','Reverb stacking means using two or more reverbs at once on a sound. Each one adds its own echo. Together they build a richer, deeper space than one reverb alone.','It exists to create denser, more dimensional ambience by combining different reverb types, for example a short room plus a long hall, giving the engineer more control over depth and texture than a single algorithm allows.','A mix engineer sends a vocal to two reverb returns, a tight plate for early body and a long hall for tail, then blends their levels to place the voice in a custom space.','Reverb technique',ARRAY['Serial reverb','Short-long reverb layering','Reverb tail equalization','Pre-delay','Early reflections']::text[],ARRAY['Stacking several long reverbs until the mix becomes cluttered and words or transients are masked','Leaving all stacked reverbs full-range so low-frequency buildup muddies the mix instead of high-passing each return','Assuming more reverbs always sounds bigger, when phase and timing conflicts can actually thin the result']::text[],ARRAY['Layering a short room and a long hall on lead vocals for depth','Combining a plate and a spring reverb on a snare for character','Building an ambient pad texture from several stacked reverbs on a synth']::text[],'415df21a-f4ef-4337-84b3-c847231c6d62'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='b6d29445-7c11-5794-b925-415b39d34b35' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Reverb stacking'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
