WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='ebd4a33e-1e43-57ea-8869-6a4bfb9d0a8c' AND gt.achievement_id='92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '83067fba-a8c8-542f-b842-309178ad7e84','Partial spot alignment','Delaying a spot microphone by only part of the time offset needed to match the main array''s arrival time, rather than fully aligning it. Partial alignment trades some precedence control for a subtly closer, more present perspective on the spotted source, leaving it slightly ahead of full time-coincidence with the main pickup.','Spot mics are usually delayed to match the main mics. Partial alignment means adding only some of that delay. The spot mic stays a little early on purpose. This keeps the instrument feeling close and present, not pushed far back.','It exists to let an engineer balance presence against precedence, keeping a spotted instrument forward without fully collapsing it into the main array''s timing.','An engineer dials in less than the full geometric delay on a soloist''s spot mic, listening for the point where it stays present but does not yank the image.','Time alignment',ARRAY['Perceptual spot delay','Nearest-main delay reference','Spot microphone','Precedence effect','Orchestral precedence shift']::text[],ARRAY['Assuming alignment is all-or-nothing and never trying an intermediate delay.','Leaving too little delay, so the spot still pulls the image toward itself.','Confusing partial alignment with simply raising the spot mic''s level.']::text[],ARRAY['Keeping a concerto soloist present while a main pair holds the orchestra.','Adding presence to a lead line in a live ensemble recording.','Balancing a featured woodwind against a delayed section mic.']::text[],'92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='83067fba-a8c8-542f-b842-309178ad7e84' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Partial spot alignment'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '83067fba-a8c8-542f-b842-309178ad7e84','92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='83067fba-a8c8-542f-b842-309178ad7e84' AND gt.achievement_id='92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '272bc1cf-adf7-5c8f-b958-66a6339a84d6','Perceptual spot delay','A delay applied to a spot microphone based on how the ear perceives arrival timing—exploiting the precedence effect—rather than on exact geometric path length. The value is tuned by listening so the spot mic falls just behind the main array within the fusion window, reinforcing the source without shifting its localization.','A spot mic can be delayed to line up with the main mics. Instead of using pure math, engineers often tune the delay by ear. They set it so the close mic arrives just after the main mics. This works with how hearing localizes sound, so the image stays in place.','It exists to align support mics by perceptual result rather than raw distance, giving reinforcement that fuses with the main array and holds the correct image.','An engineer nudges a spot mic''s delay while listening until the instrument reinforces without moving, rather than trusting a distance calculation alone.','Time alignment',ARRAY['Precedence effect','Nearest-main delay reference','Partial spot alignment','Spot microphone','Orchestral precedence shift']::text[],ARRAY['Trusting only a distance-based delay figure and never checking the result by ear.','Setting the delay so the spot leads the main array, which pulls the image.','Pushing past the roughly 40 ms fusion window, creating an audible echo.']::text[],ARRAY['Tuning a soloist spot-mic delay by ear against a Decca Tree.','Aligning section mics perceptually in an orchestral mix.','Fine-tuning support-mic timing in a live classical broadcast.']::text[],'92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='272bc1cf-adf7-5c8f-b958-66a6339a84d6' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Perceptual spot delay'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '272bc1cf-adf7-5c8f-b958-66a6339a84d6','92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='272bc1cf-adf7-5c8f-b958-66a6339a84d6' AND gt.achievement_id='92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '0ce59a17-90f3-55f8-92d2-9d87ba050993','Perspective mismatch','An audible inconsistency in perceived distance or room perspective when microphones capturing the same ensemble sit at very different distances—typically close spot mics against a distant main array—so a spotted instrument sounds unnaturally near and dry compared with the surrounding, more reverberant pickup. It breaks the unified sense of the ensemble occupying one acoustic space.','Close mics make an instrument sound near and dry. Distant main mics make the group sound far and roomy. Mixing them can make one instrument seem to sit in a different space. That clash is a perspective mismatch. Engineers blend levels and reverb to fix it.','Recognizing it helps engineers blend close and distant mics so every source seems to share one room and one front-to-back perspective.','When a spotlit instrument sounds too forward against a roomy orchestra, an engineer lowers the spot, adds matching reverb or delay, or backs the mic off to restore a consistent perspective.','Ensemble miking',ARRAY['Spot microphone','Main microphone array','Natural-reverb return','Proximity effect','Ambience microphone']::text[],ARRAY['Pushing spot mics loud for detail until instruments sound detached from the hall.','Adding no reverb or delay to close mics, leaving them dry against a roomy main pair.','Placing spot mics far too close, exaggerating proximity effect and shrinking the perceived space.']::text[],ARRAY['A close-miked soloist sounding dry and forward over a distant orchestra.','Blending close instrument mics with far room mics on a live ensemble.','Matching a spot-miked choir soloist to the surrounding choir sound.']::text[],'92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='0ce59a17-90f3-55f8-92d2-9d87ba050993' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Perspective mismatch'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '0ce59a17-90f3-55f8-92d2-9d87ba050993','92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='0ce59a17-90f3-55f8-92d2-9d87ba050993' AND gt.achievement_id='92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '15b1db75-ab4e-5bf5-aa9e-b4c2b871c604','Pit-lip microphone','A microphone placed at the lip (front edge) of an orchestra pit—the rail dividing the sunken pit from the stage and audience—to capture a general or sectional blend of the pit ensemble, typically in musical theatre. Mounted at this fixed, low-profile position, it picks up the overall sound from the front of the pit while staying clear of players, the conductor, and sightlines.','An orchestra pit is a sunken area in front of a stage where the band plays. The pit lip is its front edge. A mic here catches the whole band from the front. It stays out of the players'' way.','It provides a fixed, unobtrusive pickup of the pit ensemble from the front edge, giving engineers a general full-pit or section signal for reinforcement or recording.','A theatre sound tech clamps a small condenser or boundary mic to the pit rail at the lip to reinforce or record the orchestra without blocking the audience''s view of the stage.','Pit miking',ARRAY['Pit-overhead microphone','Pit-wall boundary microphone','Boundary microphone','Spot microphone','Ensemble miking']::text[],ARRAY['Relying on a single pit-lip mic for a large pit, so quiet inner instruments get buried under brass and percussion.','Placing it where the conductor or players knock it, adding handling noise and position shifts.','UNSAFE: Running mic cables across pit steps, aisles, or walkways as trip hazards; dress and tape cables to code so performers and crew cannot trip in the dark.']::text[],ARRAY['Reinforcing a musical-theatre pit orchestra during a live show.','Capturing a general pit blend for an archival cast recording.','Providing a fallback full-pit mic alongside individual section spot mics.']::text[],'92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='15b1db75-ab4e-5bf5-aa9e-b4c2b871c604' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Pit-lip microphone'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '15b1db75-ab4e-5bf5-aa9e-b4c2b871c604','92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='15b1db75-ab4e-5bf5-aa9e-b4c2b871c604' AND gt.achievement_id='92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '809c8fea-34a8-554a-99e7-68e29fd9a944','Pit-overhead microphone','A microphone suspended or stand-mounted above an orchestra pit and aimed downward at the players to capture an overall or sectional blend of the pit ensemble from overhead. Working above the players, it picks up a natural mix of nearby instruments while keeping the mic out of the performers'' working space.','An orchestra pit sits below the front of a stage. An overhead mic hangs above the players, pointing down. It catches a natural blend of the instruments below it. It stays above everyone''s hands and stands.','It captures a blended overhead pickup of the pit that supplements close spot mics, adding a sense of the whole section from above without crowding the players.','An engineer flies or stands a condenser over a group of pit players—say the strings or reeds—and aims it down to blend that section for reinforcement or recording.','Pit miking',ARRAY['Pit-lip microphone','Pit-wall boundary microphone','Overhead microphone','Spot microphone','Ensemble miking']::text[],ARRAY['Hanging it too high, so it captures more room and audience noise than the intended instruments.','Placing one overhead over a mixed group and expecting clean separation between loud and quiet instruments.','UNSAFE: Suspending a mic and cable over players without rated hardware and a safety; secure all overhead mounts per rigging practice.']::text[],ARRAY['Blending a string section in a crowded theatre pit.','Adding overhead depth to close-miked woodwinds in a pit orchestra.','Capturing a section overview for a live-to-broadcast musical.']::text[],'92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='809c8fea-34a8-554a-99e7-68e29fd9a944' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Pit-overhead microphone'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '809c8fea-34a8-554a-99e7-68e29fd9a944','92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='809c8fea-34a8-554a-99e7-68e29fd9a944' AND gt.achievement_id='92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'd9131827-4e20-56da-a15b-8443966f6c11','Pit-wall boundary microphone','A boundary (pressure-zone) microphone mounted flush against a wall or other large rigid surface of an orchestra pit, using that surface as an acoustic boundary. Placing the capsule at the boundary avoids the comb-filter cancellations that a delayed surface reflection would cause and yields up to about 6 dB of pressure buildup, giving a clean hemispherical pickup of the pit.','A boundary mic sits flat against a hard surface, like a pit wall. Being right on the wall stops the echo off that wall from smearing the sound. It also makes the signal a bit louder. The result is a clean pickup of the pit.','It exploits the pit wall as a boundary to give a low-profile, reflection-free pickup with extra sensitivity, useful where a stand-mounted mic would be in the way.','A tech tapes or mounts a boundary mic flat on a large section of pit wall or a hard baffle to capture the ensemble without a visible stand or hanging mic.','Pit miking',ARRAY['Boundary microphone','Pit-lip microphone','Pit-overhead microphone','Comb filtering','Pressure zone microphone']::text[],ARRAY['Mounting it on a small, thin, or vibrating panel instead of a large rigid surface, losing the boundary benefit and adding structure-borne noise.','Expecting full low-frequency pressure buildup when the boundary surface is smaller than the low-frequency wavelength.','UNSAFE: Connecting or disconnecting the mic with phantom power live, which can send a loud thump to speakers; mute the channel and let phantom power settle first.']::text[],ARRAY['Capturing a pit orchestra where stands and hanging mics would block sightlines.','Using a hard pit baffle as a boundary for a discreet section pickup.','Adding a low-profile ensemble mic in a shallow or crowded pit.']::text[],'92eda6e7-32ec-432c-9bd3-18bfe726233f'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='d9131827-4e20-56da-a15b-8443966f6c11' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Pit-wall boundary microphone'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
